@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+Use case: stylized-concept.
+Asset type: one proposed Fengjia Night Market landmark cutout for a north-up Game Boy Advance overworld. This is an architecture study for user review, not a completed native tileset.
+
+Input images and roles: Image 1 is an official real Fengjia Night Market photograph: use its distinctive lightweight, gently wavy rainbow street-entry sign, four yellow stars, white circular end panels, and adjacent compact urban shopfronts as the architectural identity. Image 2 is another official photo of this same night market: use the dense narrow shopfront rhythm, practical fabric/metal awnings, simple merchandise counters and horizontal/vertical signboard shapes. Image 3 is ONLY the approved native Old Taichung Station sprite: match its crisp square-pixel rendering, frontal north-up RPG perspective, slightly visible roof depth, dark outlines and flat shade clusters. Do not copy the station architecture, brickwork, tower, symmetry, palette, glow or any wording from any reference.
+
+Primary request: a compact, recognizable slice of the actual Wenhua Road Fengjia market entry, translated into a south-facing RPG landmark. Two short, tightly packed low-rise modern shopfront blocks flank one central ground-level pedestrian-market portal. The real colorful lightweight entry banner stretches across the central gap, with its shallow wavy silhouette, four small yellow stars above the left half and large white circular panels near its ends. Keep the colorful horizontal bands but leave the entire sign's lettering area BLANK for later hand-authored localization. Its support is ordinary slim metal between the shopfronts, not a monumental stone gateway. Show a few simple cream/gray concrete shop walls, dark upper rectangular windows, projecting blank sign boxes, red/yellow practical awnings, and small simplified counters. The single central south-facing portal at the bottom baseline is a clear dark rectangular opening, representing the market lane as the game's future gym entrance; no other active-looking doors. Preserve the real street-market character rather than inventing a palace, temple, pagoda, station or shopping-mall gate.
+
+Composition: one cohesive shallow landmark cutout, front baseline exactly horizontal, centered with generous fully transparent padding. North is up; roof depth is visible only as shallow top planes on the adjacent low-rise shops, never a diagonal isometric diorama or perspective vanishing-point street. Compress the actual depth into this readable front-facing sprite. Provisional logical canvas 192x128 native pixels, each logical square pixel enlarged exactly 8x, making a 1536x1024 PNG if possible. Keep bold coherent blocks and a legible ground portal near the horizontal center at the bottom of the building silhouette. Footprint and native door binding will be reconciled separately; do not draw a map around it.
+
+Rendering: strict GBA-style pixel art, a single square pixel grid, hard edges, 14 opaque flat colors plus transparency, no dithering. Use charcoal outlines; cream/gray concrete; a restrained red, golden yellow, blue/cyan and green sign/awning family. Local sign faces and counters may have a light cream highlight to suggest the night-market identity, but use ordinary daylight-compatible materials and no global night tint. Every drawn pixel is fully opaque; the empty background and holes are truly transparent.
+
+Avoid: all text, letters, numbers, Chinese characters, simulated glyphs, shop logos, brand mascots, watermarks, people, Pokemon, vehicles, power cables, food photography, surrounding ground, pavement, scenery, sky, trees, detached props, UI, checkerboard drawn into the image, gradients, antialiasing, blur, soft glow, cast shadows, painterly texture, extra ornate towers or roofs. Do not turn the rainbow banner into the separate Fun Star shopping-gate design.
